      "id": 2,
      "claim": "When the AS curve is upward‑sloping, a rightward shift of aggregate demand (AD) moves the equilibrium to a point with a higher price level **and** a higher real GDP.",
      "evidence": "Comparative‑static analysis of the AD‑AS model: with AS having a positive slope (dY/dP > 0), an increase in AD raises the intersection price P* and output Y* because both variables must adjust to satisfy the new AD level; the direction of change follows the sign of the slope (Mankiw, Ch. 23, Fig. 23‑3).",
      "weight": 0.5,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Therefore, operating in the intermediate range, an increase in aggregate demand results in an increase in both the price level and real GDP.",
      "evidence": "Logical synthesis of steps 1 and 2.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [2]
    }
  ],
